# Stream and Elicit

Two skills every real agent needs: streaming a large read lazily instead of
materializing it, and pausing a task with `:input_required` when the caller
hasn't supplied enough to act on — then resuming the same task once they do.

## Setup

```elixir
Mix.install([{:ash, "~> 3.0"}, {:ash_a2a, "~> 26.9.31"}],
  consolidate_protocols: false,
  config: [
    ash: [
      validate_domain_resource_inclusion?: false,
      validate_domain_config_inclusion?: false
    ]
  ]
)
```

## Define a ticket resource and agent

```elixir
defmodule Tutorial.Ticket do
  use Ash.Resource,
    domain: Tutorial.Domain,
    data_layer: Ash.DataLayer.Ets,
    extensions: [AshA2A]

  attributes do
    uuid_primary_key(:id)
    attribute(:subject, :string, allow_nil?: false, public?: true)
    attribute(:priority, :string, default: "normal", public?: true)
  end

  actions do
    defaults([:read])

    create :create do
      primary? true
      accept([:subject])
    end
  end

  a2a do
    skill(:create_ticket, :create, description: "Create a ticket")
    skill(:list_tickets, :read)
  end
end

defmodule Tutorial.Domain do
  use Ash.Domain, extensions: [AshA2A]

  resources do
    resource(Tutorial.Ticket)
  end
end

defmodule Tutorial.TicketAgent do
  use AshA2A.Agent,
    resource_or_domain: Tutorial.Domain,
    name: "tutorial_tickets",
    public_skills: ["list_tickets"]
end

{:ok, _pid} = Tutorial.TicketAgent.start_link([])
```

Seed a few tickets. (We create them with plain Ash here to keep this
livebook focused; creating through the agent needs an authority grant, as
[Secure Your Agent](secure-your-agent.livemd) shows.)

```elixir
for subject <- ["Disk full on db-1", "Token expiry storms", "Flaky SSE reconnects"] do
  Ash.create!(Tutorial.Ticket, %{subject: subject})
end

{:ok, _apps} = Application.ensure_all_started(:ash_a2a)

user = AshA2A.Identity.principal(%{id: "user-42", tenant: "acme"})
{:ok, _authority} = AshA2A.Authority.Grant.grant(user, "Tutorial.Ticket.create")

# Every consequential call carries the verified identity in metadata and
# must match its grant; tasks are owned by the principal that created them,
# so resumes must present the same identity.
auth = %{id: "user-42", tenant: "acme"}

# Consequential calls present their verified identity as *call options* --
# the same thing AshA2A.Protocol.Plug.Auth injects on the wire.
ticket_msg = fn input ->
  AshA2A.Protocol.Message.new_user([AshA2A.Protocol.Part.Data.new(input)])
  |> struct!(metadata: %{"skill" => "create_ticket"})
end

ticket_opts = [metadata: %{"a2a.auth" => %{identity: auth}}]
```

## Stream a read

A Data part carrying `"stream" => true` on a `:read` skill produces a lazy
per-record stream — the query runs through `Ash.stream!/2` and one
`Part.Data` arrives per record (the record map itself), instead of one
materialized list. The task stays `:working` while the caller consumes and
completes when the stream ends.

```elixir
stream_msg =
  AshA2A.Protocol.Message.new_user([AshA2A.Protocol.Part.Data.new(%{"stream" => true})])
  |> struct!(metadata: %{"skill" => "list_tickets"})

{:ok, task, stream} = AshA2A.Protocol.stream(Tutorial.TicketAgent, stream_msg)

subjects =
  stream
  |> Enum.map(& &1.data.subject)
  |> Enum.sort()

{:ok, done} = Tutorial.TicketAgent.get_task(task.id)
done.status.state
#=> :completed
```

## Elicit: pause for missing input, then resume

Send a `create_ticket` message with no subject. The Ash action fails with a
missing-argument error, which the dispatcher maps to `{:input_required, parts}`
— the task pauses in `:input_required` instead of failing, with a short,
actionable prompt in `status.message`.

```elixir
incomplete_msg = ticket_msg.(%{})

{:ok, task} = AshA2A.Protocol.call(Tutorial.TicketAgent, incomplete_msg, ticket_opts)

task.status.state
#=> :input_required

task.status.message
|> AshA2A.Protocol.Message.text()
|> String.contains?("attribute subject is required")
#=> true
```

Resume the same task by sending a new message with `task_id:`. The full
conversation history rides along in the task, so the agent sees both turns.

```elixir
{:ok, task} =
  AshA2A.Protocol.call(Tutorial.TicketAgent, ticket_msg.(%{"subject" => "Disk full on db-2"}),
    task_id: task.id,
    metadata: %{"a2a.auth" => %{identity: auth}}
  )

task.status.state
#=> :completed

[%AshA2A.Protocol.Part.Data{data: %{subject: "Disk full on db-2"}}] =
  hd(task.artifacts).parts
```

## What you built, and what's next

You now have an agent that streams large reads lazily per record, and turns
caller-fixable input problems into resumable `:input_required` tasks instead
of dead ends.

From here:

- [Secure Your Agent](secure-your-agent.livemd) if you landed here first —
  streaming and eliciting both inherit its authentication and authority
  rules;
- [A2A endpoint contract](../docs/reference/a2a-endpoint-contract.md) for how
  these map onto the wire (`message/stream` SSE frames,
  `TASK_STATE_INPUT_REQUIRED`, resume-by-task-id);
- [Message lifecycle](../docs/explanation/message-lifecycle.md) for the
  dispatch-to-receipt pipeline underneath it all.
